@* ===== Destinations: full card grid of 12 top sites ===== *@
<section class="section" id="destinations">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="section-head">
            <span class="eyebrow">Destinations</span>
            <h2>Where to go in Armenia</h2>
            <p class="lead">
                From the capital to the far south, these are the places that define a first trip.
                Most are within two to four hours' drive of Yerevan.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card-grid">
            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/yerevan-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/yerevan-700.webp 700w, /images/yerevan-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Cascade Complex staircase and fountains in Yerevan" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Capital</span>
                    <h3>Yerevan</h3>
                    <p>Armenia's capital and largest city is built of rose-coloured tuff stone. Start at Republic Square, climb the Cascade Complex for views of Mount Ararat, browse the weekend Vernissage craft market, and visit the Cafesjian Center for the Arts.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/garni-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/garni-700.webp 700w, /images/garni-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Ionic columns of Garni Temple in warm evening light" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Kotayk</span>
                    <h3>Garni Temple</h3>
                    <p>A 1st-century AD Greco-Roman temple with Ionic columns, standing on a promontory above the Azat river gorge. It is the only surviving Greco-Roman peristyle temple in Armenia and the wider region — and a striking pagan survivor in a Christian land.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/geghard-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/geghard-700.webp 700w, /images/geghard-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Rock-cut chapel interior of Geghard Monastery" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Kotayk · UNESCO World Heritage</span>
                    <h3>Geghard Monastery</h3>
                    <p>Medieval monastery in the Upper Azat Valley, with chapels hewn directly into the cliff. Its name means "spear" — the monastery once held the lance said to have pierced Christ. Listed by UNESCO for its exceptional rock-cut architecture.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/sevan-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/sevan-700.webp 700w, /images/sevan-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Sunset over Lake Sevan with mountains in the distance" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Gegharkunik</span>
                    <h3>Lake Sevan</h3>
                    <p>One of the largest high-altitude freshwater lakes in the world, about 1,900 m above sea level. Visit the 9th-century Sevanavank monastery on the peninsula, try grilled Sevan trout, and swim from the beaches in July and August.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/tatev-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/tatev-700.webp 700w, /images/tatev-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Wings of Tatev cable car cabin crossing the Vorotan gorge" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Syunik</span>
                    <h3>Tatev Monastery &amp; Wings of Tatev</h3>
                    <p>A 9th-century monastery and one-time medieval university on the rim of the Vorotan gorge. Reach it on the Wings of Tatev cableway, 5.75 km long — recognised by Guinness World Records in 2010 as the longest non-stop double-track cable car in the world.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/khor-virap-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/khor-virap-700.webp 700w, /images/khor-virap-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Khor Virap monastery in front of Mount Ararat" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Ararat</span>
                    <h3>Khor Virap</h3>
                    <p>A monastery close to the Turkish border with Armenia's most famous view: Mount Ararat rising behind its walls. Tradition holds that St Gregory the Illuminator was imprisoned here in a pit for 13 years before converting King Tiridates III to Christianity.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/noravank-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/noravank-700.webp 700w, /images/noravank-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Noravank monastery against red cliffs in Vayots Dzor" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Vayots Dzor</span>
                    <h3>Noravank</h3>
                    <p>A 13th-century monastery set at the end of a narrow gorge of brick-red cliffs. The two-storey Surb Astvatsatsin church, with its narrow external staircase, is one of the finest works of medieval Armenian architecture.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/dilijan-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/dilijan-700.webp 700w, /images/dilijan-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Forested hills and wooden balconies of Dilijan" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Tavush</span>
                    <h3>Dilijan</h3>
                    <p>A forested spa town nicknamed the "Armenian Switzerland", surrounded by Dilijan National Park. Walk the restored Sharambeyan Street of craft workshops, then hike to the Haghartsin and Goshavank monasteries hidden in the woods.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/etchmiadzin-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/etchmiadzin-700.webp 700w, /images/etchmiadzin-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Etchmiadzin Cathedral, the mother church of the Armenian Apostolic Church" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Armavir · UNESCO World Heritage</span>
                    <h3>Etchmiadzin</h3>
                    <p>Vagharshapat, better known as Etchmiadzin, is the spiritual centre of the Armenian Apostolic Church. Its cathedral, founded in the early 4th century, is considered one of the oldest cathedrals in the world and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/gyumri-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/gyumri-700.webp 700w, /images/gyumri-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="19th-century black tuff facades in the old town of Gyumri" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Shirak</span>
                    <h3>Gyumri</h3>
                    <p>Armenia's second-largest city and its traditional capital of crafts and humour. The Kumayri historic district preserves 19th-century black and orange tuff houses; rebuilt after the 1988 earthquake, the city is today known for its galleries and bakeries.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/jermuk-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/jermuk-700.webp 700w, /images/jermuk-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Jermuk waterfall and the surrounding mountain spa town" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Vayots Dzor</span>
                    <h3>Jermuk</h3>
                    <p>A mountain spa town famous for its hot mineral springs and bottled mineral water. Drink from the gallery of warm fountains, walk to the 70-metre Jermuk waterfall, and ride the ski lift for views over the Arpa gorge.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
                <figure><img src="~/images/areni-700.webp"
                         srcset="/images/areni-700.webp 700w, /images/areni-1400.webp 1400w"
                         sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 351px, (min-width: 768px) 46vw, 92vw"
                         width="1584" height="990" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                         alt="Vineyards near the village of Areni in Vayots Dzor" /></figure>
                <div class="card__body">
                    <span class="card__region">Vayots Dzor</span>
                    <h3>Areni</h3>
                    <p>The heart of Armenian wine country. Nearby, the Areni-1 cave holds the world's oldest known winery — a wine press and fermentation jars dating to roughly 4100 BC. Taste the local Areni Noir grape at the village wineries.</p>
                </div>
            </article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
